==Introduction==

===Abstract===

This document proposes an encoding format using codex32 for the key material that appears in a
[https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0380.mediawiki BIP-0380] descriptor <code>KEY</code> expression.
Each kind of key material gets its own human-readable part and its own serialization. It depends on the codex32 HRP
generalization, since BIP-0093 currently fixes the HRP as <code>ms</code>.

===Copyright===

This document is licensed under the 3-clause BSD license.

===Motivation===

A descriptor <code>KEY</code> expression carries a hex public key, a WIF private key, or a base58 extended key, any
of which may have to survive being copied by hand, printed, photographed, or read aloud. None of those formats has
codex32's guarantees and properties: a single mistyped character in an xpub or in its key origin information either
fails opaquely or silently resolves to different, valid key material. Base58's checksum detects errors but cannot
locate or correct them, and neither base58 nor hex can be split into shares.

Codex32 supplies error detection, error correction and Shamir secret sharing for the same material, and, because an
uppercase codex32 string is drawn from QR's alphanumeric character set while base58 is not, it encodes an extended
key to a QR symbol one version smaller than base58 does, despite being longer as text (see Rationale).

==Specification==

===Serialization===

Each payload below is the key material with every bit that the HRP already implies removed. The base58 version bytes
go, and so does the <code>0x00</code> that BIP-0032 places in front of a private key's 32-byte scalar: whether a key
is public or private is what the HRP says, so that byte never carried information in the first place.

<code>serP(K)</code> is 33 bytes, but it is 257 bits of information: 256 bits of x-coordinate and one bit of
y-coordinate parity, since the <code>0x02</code>/<code>0x03</code> prefix distinguishes exactly two cases. Compressed
public keys are therefore serialized in 257 bits wherever they appear, both bare and inside an extended key.

Payloads are a whole number of bytes only by coincidence, so each has some number of spare bits left over in its
final symbol. Since every payload here is fixed length, those bits are always present and are specified rather than
left as zero padding.

{| class="wikitable"
! HRP !! Key material !! Payload bits !! Symbols !! Spare bits !! Spare bits carry !! String length
|-
| <code>pk</code> || Public key, compressed || 257 || 52 || 3 || CRC-3 || 74
|-
| <code>pk</code> || Public key, uncompressed || 520 || 104 || 0 || — || 128
|-
| <code>xo</code> || X-only public key (BIP-0340) || 256 || 52 || 4 || CRC-4 || 74
|-
| <code>pr</code> || Private key, any network || 256 || 52 || 4 || network and compression status || 74
|-
| <code>xpub</code>/<code>tpub</code> || Extended public key || 585 || 117 || 0 || — || 143
|-
| <code>xprv</code>/<code>tprv</code> || Extended private key || 584 || 117 || 1 || parity || 143
|-
| <code>rn</code> || Root node: chain code and scalar || 512 || 103 || 3 || CRC-3, or unconstrained || 127
|}

A compressed <code>pk</code>, an <code>xo</code> and a <code>pr</code> are all 74 characters with a 2-character HRP
and a 52-symbol payload, matching <code>cl</code> and a 256-bit <code>ms</code> seed. Key material that is
interchangeable inside a script therefore has an interchangeable shape, so an implementation can assume one length.
An extended public and an extended private key are likewise the same length as each other, which is why dropping
BIP-0032's <code>0x00</code> is safe to do: it changes the spare-bit count and nothing else.

===Extended key fields===

BIP-0032's metadata is re-cut so that the fields a reader actually consults occupy whole characters, and the fields
that look like noise are left to straddle boundaries. The child number is placed immediately after depth, ahead of
the parent fingerprint, which is the reverse of BIP-0032's order:

{| class="wikitable"
! Field !! Bits !! Characters !! Contents
|-
| Depth || 5 || 1 || Values 0 to 31
|-
| Child number || 35 || 7 || Hardened flag, then 2 bits that MUST be zero, then the 31-bit index
|-
| Parent fingerprint || 32 || — || The 32-bit fingerprint, unpadded
|-
| Chain code || 256 || — || Begins at bit 72 in both the public and private forms
|-
| Key || 257 or 256 || — || <code>serP(K)</code> as 257 bits, or the scalar as 256
|}

An extended public key is 585 bits, exactly 117 symbols, with no spare bits; an extended private key is 584 bits,
the same 117 symbols, with one spare bit at the end of the payload carrying a parity of the rest. Both are 143
characters, and the chain code begins at the same bit offset in each, so a reader or an implementation can locate it
without first knowing which of the two it holds.

The first eight characters are readable: one for depth, seven for the child number. A master key, whose depth and
child number are both zero, opens with eight <code>q</code> characters and continues with more while its all-zero
fingerprint runs out, so the leading run breaks as soon as any of that metadata is non-zero. Splitting the child
number as hardened-flag, padding, index rather than right-aligning the whole 32 bits places the hardened flag at the
top of its own character, which makes it legible on its own:

{| class="wikitable"
! Child number !! Characters !! Right-aligned instead !! Left-aligned instead
|-
| unhardened, index 0 || <code>qqqqqqq</code> || <code>qqqqqqq</code> || <code>qqqqqqq</code>
|-
| hardened, index 0 || <code>sqqqqqq</code> || <code>zqqqqqq</code> || <code>sqqqqqq</code>
|-
| unhardened, index 1 || <code>qqqqqqp</code> || <code>qqqqqqp</code> || <code>qqqqqqg</code>
|-
| hardened, index 1 || <code>sqqqqqp</code> || <code>zqqqqqp</code> || <code>sqqqqqg</code>
|}

Only the split placement yields both indicators. Right-aligning the full 32 bits puts the hardened flag one position
lower, giving <code>z</code>, which signifies nothing; left-aligning preserves <code>s</code> but shifts the index so
that index 1 reads <code>g</code> and larger indices spill across two characters. The two characters this produces
are ones a bech32 user already reads: <code>q</code> and <code>p</code> are the witness versions distinguishing
bech32 from bech32m, and <code>s</code> is codex32's own secret index.

The 2 zero bits inside the child number are what buy that readability, and this document specifies them as zero
rather than as a CRC. A CRC placed before the index would mix into the hardened flag's character and destroy the
<code>q</code>/<code>s</code> distinction; placed after the index it would mix into the low-order character, which is
exactly where small indices live, so common indices would no longer read as themselves. The detection it would add is
small: the codex32 checksum already detects any single-character error, and 2 further bits mainly reduce the chance
of a false correction being accepted, by a factor of four. A CRC that does not collide with either indicator costs a
further character; see Open questions.

The chain code is deliberately left to begin mid-character, at bit 72. Aligning it would cost a character and buy
nothing, since alignment serves reading and a chain code is indistinguishable from noise. For the same reason the
unpadded fingerprint sits directly against it: the two high-entropy fields are adjacent, and the readable fields are
contiguous at the front.

====Alternatives considered====

'''BIP-0032's field order, at 143 characters.''' Keeping fingerprint before child number can also reach 143
characters with the child number aligned, but only by narrowing depth to 3 bits. At that width depth stops being
aligned, because the fingerprint's first two bits share the opening character with it. That trades a depth ceiling of
7 for the loss of the field most worth reading, so this document uses the swapped order, which aligns both at a depth
ceiling of 31.

'''142 characters.''' Reachable only by narrowing depth to 3 bits ''and'' abandoning alignment entirely: 3 + 32 + 32
+ 513 is 580 bits, exactly 116 symbols. The result has no readable fields, no spare bits, and a depth ceiling of 7,
against real paths that already reach depth 6. One character is not worth it.

'''Padding the fingerprint as well, at 144 characters.''' Aligning all three metadata fields costs one character and
yields 2 spare bits that a CRC could use without colliding with either indicator. This is the option to take if
review concludes the CRC is worth more than the character.

===Private keys===

<code>pr</code> is a single HRP for every network, as <code>pk</code> is. Network and compression status live in the
payload's 4 spare bits, placed ahead of the scalar rather than after it, so that they land in the first payload
character instead of being scattered through the last one.

The first payload symbol is the scalar's most significant bit followed by the 4 status bits; the remaining 51 symbols
are the scalar's other 255 bits, which is an exact fit. Because that leading scalar bit is free, each status has two
possible first characters rather than one. The four status values are chosen so that '''an uncompressed key always
begins with a digit and a compressed key always begins with a letter''':

{| class="wikitable"
! Status !! Status bits !! First character !! WIF's leading character
|-
| mainnet, uncompressed || <code>1010</code> || <code>2</code> or <code>6</code> || <code>5</code>
|-
| mainnet, compressed || <code>0110</code> || <code>x</code> or <code>k</code> || <code>K</code> or <code>L</code>
|-
| testnet, uncompressed || <code>0101</code> || <code>9</code> or <code>4</code> || <code>9</code>
|-
| testnet, compressed || <code>1000</code> || <code>g</code> or <code>c</code> || <code>c</code>
|}

Three of the four keep WIF's own leading character as one of their two options. The digit-and-letter split is only
possible at all because the free scalar bit sits at the top of the symbol: of the five positions it could occupy,
only two leave more than one pair of adjacent values that are both digits, and the bech32 alphabet contains just nine
digits against twenty-three letters. Putting it anywhere else makes the convention unsatisfiable.

Twelve of the sixteen status values are unused, so corruption confined to these bits is caught by the encoding itself
and not only by the checksum. The two characters within each row occur with equal frequency, since the scalar's top
bit is unbiased; for comparison, WIF's own <code>K</code>/<code>L</code> pair splits about 45/55.

===Cross-status ambiguity===

Reading the status wrongly produces a valid key on the wrong network, or with the wrong compression, and therefore a
different address. Three things bound that risk, and one does not:

* Any single-character substitution anywhere in a codex32 string, including in the status character, invalidates the
  checksum. A misread status is detected, not silently accepted.
* Twelve of the sixteen status bit patterns are unused, so a corruption landing in the status bits alone is caught
  even before the checksum is consulted.
* Compression status is legible from the character class alone: a digit is uncompressed, a letter is compressed.
  A reader who recalls nothing else about the format cannot mistake one for the other, and the bech32 alphabet
  already excludes <code>1</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>i</code> and <code>o</code> for confusability.
* Hamming distance ''between symbol values'' is not a meaningful safeguard here and should not be cited as one.
  Codex32's code is over GF(32); it counts symbol errors, so a status character differing from another in one bit
  and one differing in four bits are both simply one symbol error. Bit distance matters only for the bit-level
  detection described above, which is why the status values are not chosen to maximise it.

The residual risk is mis-correction rather than misreading: a decoder correcting errors elsewhere in the string could
in principle satisfy the checksum by altering the status character instead. Implementations therefore SHOULD NOT
correct the status character, and SHOULD report a correction that would change it rather than applying it, for the
same reason BIP-0093 says implementations should not correct the human-readable part.

===Identifier===

The first 20 bits of a key's BIP-0032 fingerprint MAY be used as the default codex32 identifier, but only for
<code>xo</code>. The identifier is four bech32 characters, so a 32-bit fingerprint does not fit and must be
truncated; implementations that adopt this default MUST take the first 20 bits so that they interoperate.

For every other profile in this document the default is inappropriate, for a reason specific to each:

* <code>pk</code> and <code>pr</code>: the fingerprint is the first 20 bits of <code>hash160</code> of the public
  key, which is exactly the first 20 bits of a P2PKH or P2WPKH output's script. A single share, below the threshold
  and on its own, would then narrow the on-chain outputs it belongs to by a factor of about a million. The property
  secret sharing protects for public key material is not the secrecy of the key, which is meant to become known, but
  the unlinkability of one recovered share to a particular wallet's on-chain history. A fingerprint default would
  give that away.
* <code>xo</code> is the exception because a P2TR output commits to the tweaked output key, not to
  <code>hash160</code> of the internal key, so that hash appears nowhere on chain and correlates to nothing a
  passive observer can see.
* <code>xpub</code>, <code>tpub</code>, <code>xprv</code> and <code>tprv</code>: the fingerprint is not published on
  chain, but it is carried in descriptors and PSBTs shared with cosigners and watch-only servers, so it remains a
  cross-document correlator between a share and a wallet those parties already hold.
* <code>rn</code>: a root node's fingerprint is the master fingerprint that appears in the key origin information of
  every descriptor derived from it, making it the strongest correlator of all.

Where the default does not apply, the identifier SHOULD be chosen the way BIP-0093 already suggests, and MUST NOT be
derived from the key material.

===Generation from fresh shares===

BIP-0093's procedure for generating a fresh secret by freely choosing share payloads applies only where every payload
value of the right length is valid. That holds for <code>rn</code> if its spare bits are left unconstrained, which is
why this document permits that alternative, and it holds for no other profile here:

* <code>pk</code>, <code>xo</code>, <code>xpub</code> and <code>tpub</code> embed a public key that must be on the
  curve, which a freely chosen payload essentially never is.
* <code>xprv</code> and <code>tprv</code> additionally fix depth, parent fingerprint and child number, which a freely
  chosen payload will not satisfy.
* <code>pk</code>, <code>xo</code> and the extended keys constrain their spare bits to a CRC or parity of the rest of
  the payload, which a freely chosen final symbol will not match.
* <code>pr</code> constrains its spare bits to one of four status patterns.

For these profiles a fresh key MUST be generated first and then split, rather than assembled from freely chosen
shares.

==Rationale==

'''Why one HRP for private keys instead of <code>wif</code> and <code>twif</code>?''' A 4-character <code>twif</code>
would have the same shape as <code>tpub</code> and <code>tprv</code> while holding something entirely different, which
works against letting implementations infer length from the HRP. <code>pk</code> already covers every network with one
HRP, and a private key is what a public key is derived from, so <code>pr</code> alongside <code>pk</code> keeps both
the shape and the pairing. It is unregistered in SLIP-0173.

'''Why not a dedicated status character?''' Spending a 53rd payload symbol would make the first character fully
determined, so the four statuses could be exactly <code>5</code>, <code>k</code>, <code>9</code> and <code>c</code>,
matching WIF's leading characters outright while still reading digit for uncompressed and letter for compressed, and
would free all 4 spare bits for a CRC-4. It costs one character and, because 74 and 75 characters fall in the same QR
version at every error-correction level, that character is free in QR terms. It is not free in shape terms: it would
make a private key one character longer than the compressed public key it is interchangeable with. This document
keeps the shapes matched, and records the alternative as an open question rather than settling it, because it is the
stronger option on every axis except that one.

'''What WIF already achieves.''' WIF, introduced by Pieter Wuille in 2011, base58check-encodes a version byte
(<code>0x80</code> mainnet, <code>0xEF</code> testnet), the scalar, and an appended <code>0x01</code> when the key is
compressed. Because base58's leading character follows from the leading bytes' numeric range, both facts are legible
from the first character: <code>5</code> and <code>9</code> for uncompressed mainnet and testnet at 51 characters,
<code>K</code>/<code>L</code> and <code>c</code> for compressed at 52. Note that WIF's own indicator is a pair, not a
single character, and an unequal one: <code>K</code> and <code>L</code> occur in roughly a 45/55 split.

This document keeps WIF's character as one of the two options in three of the four cases, and is more legible than
WIF in two respects. The status is a specified field rather than a side effect of where a number happens to fall,
so corrupting it is detectable; and compression is readable from the character class, digit against letter, rather
than from four memorised characters. It cannot reproduce <code>K</code> and <code>L</code> as a pair, because the
two options for any status differ in exactly one bit of the symbol and those two characters differ in three.

'''Version bytes versus an indicator symbol.''' Bech32-era formats replaced version bytes with a witness version
symbol, as checksums moved from truncated SHA-256 to BCH. The choice here is accordingly between constraining spare
bits that already exist and spending a character, not between a version byte and anything else.

'''QR size.''' An uppercase codex32 string is entirely within QR's alphanumeric character set, at 5.5 bits per
character, where byte mode costs 8. Measured with a QR encoder rather than estimated, at error-correction level L:

{| class="wikitable"
! Key material !! This document !! QR version !! Existing format !! QR version
|-
| Extended key || 143 characters || 5 || 111 base58 characters || 6
|-
| Public key, compressed || 74 characters || 3 || 66 hex characters || 4 lowercase, 3 uppercase
|-
| X-only public key || 74 characters || 3 || 64 hex characters || 4 lowercase, 3 uppercase
|-
| Public key, uncompressed || 128 characters || 5 || 130 hex characters || 6 lowercase, 5 uppercase
|-
| Private key || 74 characters || 3 || 52 WIF characters || 3
|}

The saving against base58 is real and cannot be taken back: base58's alphabet is mixed case, so a base58 string can
never use alphanumeric mode, and an extended key is a version smaller here despite being 33 characters longer as
text.

The saving against hex is conditional and should not be claimed without qualification. Descriptors write hex in
lower case, which forces byte mode, but hex is case-insensitive as a value, so an encoder is free to upper-case a
bare key before encoding it and land in the same version as codex32. Against lower-case hex as actually written,
codex32 saves a version; against an encoder that upper-cases first, it ties.

The private key ties with WIF, and no arrangement of this format beats it. 74 alphanumeric characters is 407 data
bits against WIF's 416, so codex32 is already the smaller payload, but both fall inside version 3 at every
error-correction level. Version 2 holds 47 alphanumeric characters, and a 256-bit scalar alone is 52 symbols, so even
discarding the checksum and the header entirely leaves 52 characters, still inside version 3. There is no version to
be won here: WIF at 52 characters sits one byte under version 3's 53-byte capacity, and a 52-symbol payload sits
25 characters under version 3's 77-character capacity.

==Backwards compatibility==

These are new HRPs under the generalized-HRP codex32 grammar. Nothing changes for existing <code>ms</code> or
<code>cl</code> strings, for BIP-0380 descriptors, or for WIF, hex and base58 serializations, which remain valid
inputs wherever they are accepted today.

==Open questions==

* Whether to spend a 53rd symbol on a dedicated status character, buying exact parity with WIF's leading characters
  and a CRC-4, at the cost of the shape match with <code>pk</code>. It is free in QR terms, and is the stronger
  option on every other axis, so the shape match is the whole of the argument against it.
* Whether restricting depth to 0-31 is acceptable, or whether it should be padded to 10 bits so that every BIP-0032
  depth remains representable. That costs one further character and is also free in QR terms.
* Key origin information: a bracketed BIP-0380 prefix outside the codex32 string, or folded into the HRP. Folding is
  the larger QR saving, since origin information dominates a cosigner-heavy descriptor, but a bare key is where the
  one-version saving above comes from, and an encoder that does not segment loses much of it.
* Whether <code>rn</code> should constrain its spare bits to a CRC-3 or leave them unconstrained. Only the
  unconstrained form can be produced by BIP-0093's fresh-share procedure.
* Whether the extended-key HRPs should stay 4 characters, given that the same reasoning that rejected
  <code>twif</code> applies to telling <code>xprv</code> and <code>tprv</code> apart from each other by content
  rather than by shape.

